lue_high": ""}], "**3.66% APY [\\*].**\u001fBring at least $25,000 [\\*] in new money or funds from a matured Dupaco certificate to earn an\u00a0introductory\u00a0**4.00% dividend rate**\u00a0for six months.": [{"kind": "offer", "product": "", "rate_type": "APY", "term": "", "tier": "", "value": "3.66%", "intro": true, "expired": false, "bound": "exact", "value_high": ""}], "As of, 8/24/26, the tiered introductory rate structure is as follows: $0.00-$24,999.99 at 0.00% dividend rate and 0.00% APY; $25,000.00-99,999.99 at 4.00% dividend rate and 3.66% APY; $100,000.00-$249,999.99 at 4.00% dividend rate and 3.66% APY; $250,000- $1,000,000.00 at 4.00% dividend rate and 3.66% APY.\u001fEligible Term-Share Certificate Maturity Funds are defined as funds from a\u202fDupaco\u202fCommunity Credit Union Term-Share Certificate that matured not more than 10 calendar days prior to depositing them into the Achieve Money Market Account. The minimum balance to earn the APY is $25,000.00.": [{"kind": "offer", "product": "Achieve Money Market Account", "rate_type": "dividend rate", "term": "", "tier": "$0.00-$24,999.99", "value": "0.00%", "intro": true, "expired": false, "bound": "exact", "value_high": ""}, {"kind": "offer", "product": "Achieve Money Market Account", "rate_type": "APY", "term": "", "tier": "$0.00-$24,999.99", "value": "0.00%", "intro": true, "expired": false, "bound": "exact", "value_high": ""}, {"kind": "offer", "product": "Achieve Money Market Account", "rate_type": "dividend rate", "term": "", "tier": "$25,000.00-99,999.99", "value": "4.00%", "intro": true, "expired": false, "bound": "exact", "value_high": ""}, {"kind": "offer", "product": "Achieve Money Market Account", "rate_type": "APY", "term": "", "tier": "$25,000.00-99,999.99", "value": "3.66%", "intro": true, "expired": false, "bound": "exact", "value_high": ""}, {"kind": "offer", "product": "Achieve Money Market Account", "rate_type": "dividend rate", "term": "", "tier": "$100,000.00-$249,999.99", "value": "4.00%", "intro": true, "expired": false, "bound": "exact", "value_high": ""}, {"kind": "offer", "product": "Achieve Money Market Account", "rate_type": "APY", "term": "", "tier": "$100,000.00-$249,999.99", "value": "3.66%", "intro": true, "expired": false, "bound": "exact", "value_high": ""}, {"kind": "offer", "product": "Achieve Money Market Account", "rate_type": "dividend rate", "term": "", "tier": "$250,000- $1,000,000.00", "value": "4.00%", "intro": true, "expired": false, "bound": "exact", "value_high": ""}, {"kind": "offer", "product": "Achieve Money Market Account", "rate_type": "APY", "term": "", "tier": "$250,000- $1,000,000.00", "value": "3.66%", "intro": true, "expired": false, "bound": "exact", "value_high": ""}], "Rates are based on minimum credit score of 760 and 75% loan to value (ltv).\u001fRate quotes reflect 30 day rate lock and expire at 3pm Central Time daily. Rates assume owner occupied - primary and secondary residence only.": [], "(Hint: Dupaco offers eligible borrowers fixed-rate home loans, first-time homebuyer loans, 100% financing loans and [more]!)\u001f- **Local loan servicing:** Some lenders (like Dupaco) service your loan locally\u2014meaning you\u2019ll always know who to contact if you have a question. - **Variety of loan options:** Look for a lender that offers a variety of borrowing options so you can find a loan that best fits your needs.": [], "The initial contribution rate for enrolled participants must be at least 3% (but no more than 10%).\u001fBeginning in 2025, certain employers must automatically enroll eligible employees into 401(k) or 403(b) plans established on or after Dec. 29, 2022, according to Ascensus.": [{"kind": "other", "product": "401(k) or 403(b) plans", "rate_type": "rate", "term": "", "tier": "", "value": "3%", "intro": false, "expired": false, "bound": "minimum", "value_high": ""}, {"kind": "other", "product": "401(k) or 403(b) plans", "rate_type": "rate", "term": "", "tier": "", "value": "10%", "intro": false, "expired": false, "bound": "maximum", "value_high": ""}], "At Dupaco, for example, your interest rate won\u2019t change more than 2% during each periodic adjustment.\u001fAfter that, your interest rate (and monthly loan payment) will adjust annually for the remaining 25 years of your loan based on what\u2019s happening in the market. But your interest rate can only adjust so much.": [{"kind": "offer", "product": "Dupaco\u2019s ARMs", "rate_type": "rate", "term": "each periodic adjustment", "tier": "", "value": "2%", "intro": false, "expired": false, "bound": "maximum", "value_high": ""}], "Rates can\u2019t rise more than 2% in one year or more than 6% over the life of the loan.\u001fWhat to consider carefully: - After the first five years, the second loan\u2019s interest rate can adjust every year, based on the [Secured Overnight Financing Rate].": [{"kind": "offer", "product": "second loan", "rate_type": "rate", "term": "one year", "tier": "", "value": "2%", "intro": false, "expired": false, "bound": "maximum", "value_high": ""}, {"kind": "offer", "product": "second loan", "rate_type": "rate", "term": "life of the loan", "tier": "", "value": "6%", "intro": false, "expired": false, "bound": "maximum", "value_high": ""}], "- At 9% interest: About $498/month\u001fLet\u2019s say you put $6,000 down on a $30,000 vehicle and borrow the rest\u00a0over 60 months. Here's what your monthly payment could look like at different interest rates:": [{"kind": "example", "product": "vehicle", "rate_type": "rate", "term": "60 months", "tier": "", "value": "9%", "intro": false, "expired": false, "bound": "exact", "value_high": ""}], "- At 7% interest: About $475/month, saving about $1,380 over 5 years\u001fHere's what your monthly payment could look like at different interest rates: - At 9% interest: About $498/month": [{"kind": "example", "product": "", "rate_type": "rate", "term": "5 years", "tier": "", "value": "7%", "intro": false, "expired": false, "bound": "exact", "value_high": ""}], "- At 5% interest: About $453/month, saving about $2,700 over 5 years\u001f- At 9% interest: About $498/month - At 7% interest: About $475/month, saving about $1,380 over 5 years": [{"kind": "example", "product": "car", "rate_type": "rate", "term": "5 years", "tier": "", "value": "5%", "intro": false, "expired": false, "bound": "exact", "value_high": ""}], "Automakers may also offer low-rate or 0% financing, which reduces the amount of interest you pay on your auto loan.": [{"kind": "education", "product": "auto loan", "rate_type": "rate", "term": "", "tier": "", "value": "0%", "intro": false, "expired": false, "bound": "exact", "value_high": ""}], "\u201cIn some instances, a down payment plus the rebates might figure better in the long run than 0% interest.\u201d\u001fBut never assume one deal is better than the other just because it sounds bigger or shinier. > \u201cYou\u2019ll want to compare whether a low-interest rate or rebate will be the better deal long-term,\u201d Hall said.": [{"kind": "education", "product": "", "rate_type": "rate", "term": "", "tier": "", "value": "0%", "intro": false, "expired": false, "bound": "exact", "value_high": ""}], "**Rule of thumb:** Refinancing might make sense if you can reduce your interest rate by at least **0.5%,** Frederick said.\u001f- Lower your monthly payments, giving you more breathing room in your budget. - Help you build equity in your home faster by paying down your principal more quickly.": [{"kind": "education", "product": "home loan refinancing", "rate_type": "rate", "term": "", "tier": "", "value": "0.5%", "intro": false, "expired": false, "bound": "minimum", "value_high": ""}], "- At 9% interest = $498/month\u001ftting a lower interest rate on the loan can save you a lot of money each month, and over the loan's duration.']Here\u2019s an example:\u00a0Let\u2019s say you put $6,000 down on a $30,000 car purchase and borrow over a 60-month loan term. Look at the amount you\u2019d spend each month at these different interest rates:": [{"kind": "example", "product": "car loan", "rate_type": "rate", "term": "60-month loan term", "tier": "", "value": "9%", "intro": false, "expired": false, "bound": "exact", "value_high": ""}], "- At 7% interest = $475/month, saving $1,380 over 5 years\u001fLook at the amount you\u2019d spend each month at these different interest rates: - At 9% interest = $498/month": [{"kind": "example", "product": "car", "rate_type": "rate", "term": "5 years", "tier": "", "value": "7%", "intro": false, "expired": false, "bound": "exact", "value_high": ""}], "- At 5% interest = $453/month, saving $2,700 over 5 years\u001f- At 9% interest = $498/month - At 7% interest = $475/month, saving $1,380 over 5 years": [{"kind": "example", "product": "", "rate_type": "rate", "term": "", "tier": "", "value": "5%", "intro": false, "expired": false, "bound": "exact", "value_high": ""}], "\u201cIn some instances, a down payment plus the rebates might figure better in the long run than 0% interest.\u201d\u001fYou\u2019ll want to look beyond the monthly payment that any car incentive promises. > \u201cYou\u2019ll want to compare whether a low-interest rate or rebate will be the better deal long-term,\u201d Hall said.": [{"kind": "other", "product": "", "rate_type": "rate", "term": "", "tier": "", "value": "0%", "intro": false, "expired": false, "bound": "exact", "value_high": ""}], "For example: Let\u2019s say you own a five-year bond with a 2% coupon when five-year rates are at 2%.\u001fThe price of bonds is inversely related to interest rates. An increase in interest rates will cause an equivalent drop in the price of bonds.": [{"kind": "education", "product": "five-year bond", "rate_type": "rate", "term": "five-year", "tier": "", "value": "2%", "intro": false, "expired": false, "bound": "exact", "value_high": ""}, {"kind": "education", "product": "five-year bond", "rate_type": "rate", "term": "five-year", "tier": "", "value": "2%", "intro": false, "expired": false, "bound": "exact", "value_high": ""}], "But if five-year rates go up to 3%, your 2% bond now has less value.\u001fFor example: Let\u2019s say you own a five-year bond with a 2% coupon when five-year rates are at 2%. Your bond should be priced at par.": [{"kind": "education", "product": "five-year bond", "rate_type": "rate", "term": "five-year", "tier": "", "value": "3%", "intro": false, "expired": false, "bound": "exact", "value_high": ""}, {"kind": "education", "product": "five-year bond", "rate_type": "rate", "term": "five-year", "tier": "", "value": "2%", "intro": false, "expired": false, "bound": "exact", "value_high": ""}], "> \u201cI did a quick calculation, and it appeared the interest rate was around 46%,\u201d Damme said.\u001fEven so, his balance remained largely the same\u2014thanks to the high-interest rate he was being charged. The loan stood out to Dupaco\u2019s Michelle Damme, who was reviewing Acosta\u2019s finances while working with him earlier this year.": [{"kind": "other", "product": "", "rate_type": "rate", "term": "", "tier": "", "value": "46%", "intro": false, "expired": false, "bound": "exact", "value_high": ""}], "Experts say curbstoners orchestrate up to 80% of used cars sold through online classified ads.\u001fA curbstoner often gets away with scamming buyers because he or she sells the vehicle and then disappears. With no office or contact information, a buyer can end up with a lot of headaches.": [], "(Over the life of the loan, your interest rate will stay within 6% of the original rate.)\u001f- Your interest rate stays the same for the first year. But it can adjust up to 2% annually after that.": [], "In some regions, insurance rates have increased by as much as 50%.\u001fts in recent years. - **Your homeowner\u2019s insurance premiums went up:** Many insurance carriers nationwide have increased their rates due to higher rebuilding and repair costs, more frequent and severe weather events and other economic factors that have made it more expensive to pay insurance claims.": [{"kind": "other", "product": "insurance", "rate_type": "rate", "term": "", "tier": "", "value": "50%", "intro": false, "expired": false, "bound": "up_to", "value_high": ""}], "Relief efforts include loan payment suspension and 0% interest rates on certain federal loans through Dec.\u001f- Potential loan forgiveness You also lose any emergency relief being offered for certain federal student loans during the pandemic.": [{"kind": "education", "product": "federal loans", "rate_type": "rate", "term": "through Dec.", "tier": "certain", "value": "0%", "intro": false, "expired": false, "bound": "exact", "value_high": ""}], "\u201cIt\u2019s hard to beat 0% interest and no payments.\u001fAre they federal, private or a combination of the two? > \u201cIf you have certain federal student loans, now may not be the best time to refinance, given the emergency relief measures that are currently in place,\u201d said Denise Burmeister, director of strategic partnerships at CU Student Choice.": [{"kind": "education", "product": "federal student loans", "rate_type": "rate", "term": "", "tier": "", "value": "0%", "intro": false, "expired": false, "bound": "exact", "value_high": ""}], "[Credit card] cash advances, usually offered at an annual percentage rate of 30% or less, are much cheaper than getting a payday loan.": [{"kind": "education", "product": "Credit card cash advances", "rate_type": "APR", "term": "", "tier": "", "value": "30%", "intro": false, "expired": false, "bound": "maximum", "value_high": ""}], "Your rate may adjust up to 2% every 12 months after but will stay within 6% of your original rate.\u001f![Orange graphic of an outlined checkmark.] After 12 months, your loan converts to a conventional adjustable-rate mortgage with a fixed rate for the first year.": [{"kind": "offer", "product": "conventional adjustable-rate mortgage", "rate_type": "rate", "term": "12 months", "tier": "", "value": "2%", "intro": false, "expire